     "result_text_tail": "  - private label keeps gaining share at its FY2023\u201325 pace;\n  - the tested fee ($7.99, free above $100) goes to all stores.\n- **Sizing:** sales are held at FY2025 levels with no growth, minus the store exits and the 2% e-commerce sales loss from the fee test. The FY2026 figure rests on my own phasing assumptions, shown on the roadmap slide.\n- **Not sized:** vendor funding (down from 55% to 46% of markdowns) is treated as upside only, because the pack has no markdown dollar figures.\n\n**Points to raise before the board**\n- **3.0% with little room to spare:** the plan reaches 3.02%, so it leans on the wage offset and on unsized upside such as vendor funding and the delivery contract tender.\n- **FY2026 timing:** the pack doesn't say whether FY2026 has already closed by February 2027. I followed management's draft and treated it as the first plan year. The CFO should check the covenant point against the latest FY2026 forecast.\n- **Shrink estimate:** the \"up to $10M\" assumes all of the FY2023\u201325 rise in shrink came from fewer hours. If only waste and receiving errors respond, it is about $6.5M. Both cases are on the slide.\n- **Display quirk:** the renderer shows the test's average delivery fee as \"$6.4\" instead of \"$6.40\".\n\nI can switch the theme, add a slide sizing the vendor-funding upside once you have markdown dollar figures, or change any of the targets above.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`:\n- harlow-margin-plan.pptx\n- harlow-margin-plan-sources.xlsx\n- harlow-margin-plan.json"
    }
